# DESCRIPTION

Radharc owns the screen and shows clients' windows on it.
It runs only in the desktop's images (`vx boot --desktop`, vx-build(1)), as the service `windows`, which each client that uses it reaches at `/svc/windows`.

Radharc maps the framebuffer that the boot data names, as console(4) does, and composes on the CPU into a copy in memory, copying only what changed to the framebuffer.
When it starts it takes the framebuffer from the console through the console's `handoff` (radharc-handoff(5)), and the console draws into a window from then on.

A client opens a window from `compositor` (radharc-compositor(5)), and each window is a channel of its own (radharc-window(5)).
A window's pixels are images the client shares: 32-bit `0x00RRGGBB` words, sRGB, in a VMO, each with an acquire counter that the client raises once it has drawn, and a release counter that Radharc raises once it no longer reads the image.
The client presents layers, solid colours or images at places in the window, drawn for the size of a configure Radharc sent it.
An image drawn for an older size is shown at its own size, cropped, never stretched.

An image is opaque sRGB unless the client tags it (`tagImage`): a tag names its pixels' transfer, primaries and alpha.
Radharc composes only sRGB with BT.709's primaries, and refuses a tag naming another transfer or other primaries with the error that names it (`transfer`, `primaries`).
An image tagged with alpha, premultiplied or straight, has it in each pixel's top byte, and is blended over the layers beneath it in linear light (light(2)); an opaque image, a solid layer and the decorations are copied.

Radharc composes at a vblank of its own, 60 a second, which nothing drives: the boot framebuffer has none.
A change, such as a present or the pointer moving, wakes a thread that waits for the next vblank, takes each window's oldest present whose images are drawn, composes once, and sends each of those windows a `frame` giving back the present's credit.
A window may have 1 to 3 presents queued, its latency; one more is refused as `noCredit`.
A window that is hidden, or covered by one above it, has its presents taken about once a second.
With nothing changing, the thread sleeps.
It runs under a deadline profile of 4 ms in every 16.7 ms when the manifest gives Radharc the system resource.

Windows are managed as Be's were.
A press selects a window at once, and it rises on release unless the press became a drag.
Dragging its tab moves it, its corner grip resizes it, and a moved window snaps to the screen's edges within 12 pixels.
The tab's close box asks the client to close the window, and its zoom box fills the screen less the decorations, or goes back.
A new window opens cascaded from the last and is focused.
The decorations, the desk and the cursor are drawn in the `desktop`, `window.body` and `window.tab` parts and their `.focused` states (theme(6)) of the theme in force.

Radharc serves the theme.
It reads the installed themes from `/lib/theme` when it starts, and puts Platinum Indigo in force, or the base theme if Platinum Indigo isn't installed or fails its check.
Every client's tree has the same theme tree at `theme`, and a change made through one is everyone's: `ctl` loads another theme, chooses a variant, or sets a role over the theme, as the Appearance editor does.
Each change resolves the whole stack again (theme(6)) and checks it as `theme check` does; a theme that doesn't resolve or fails the check is refused with the reason, and the theme before it stays.
A change redraws the screen, raises the theme's generation, writes a line to each reader of `events`, and makes `resolved` the new theme.
`resolved` is memory, read only, holding one message in the wire format: radharc-themefile(5)'s `resolved` event, which an app maps and reads its theme from without parsing a theme file.
A client that mapped it keeps the theme it mapped; the file is the new one's after a change, so an app maps it again when `events` tells it to.

Radharc reads the keyboard from kbdfs(4)'s `/dev/kbd` and the pointer from mouse(4)'s `/dev/mouse`.
Keys go to the focused window, with the HID usage of the key, its unshifted character and the modifiers held after it; the usage is the keymap's for the rune `/dev/kbd` names.
The pointer moves the cursor and works the windows, and the window under it hears where it is in its own content, its buttons (1 the left, 2 the right, 3 the middle) and the wheel's notches.
A window's input comes on a ring of its own, 256 events of 40 bytes, with a counter as its doorbell; a window that does not read its ring loses what does not fit.

Radharc draws the trusted prompt, which only the user's own keyboard and pointer answer.
A client whose manifest says `grant windows prompt` (factotum's does) asks a question by opening `prompt`, writing it, and reading the answer, which the read waits for; closing the file before then withdraws the question.
Questions wait in turn, the oldest on screen.
The prompt is a panel over every window, the rest of the screen dimmed to a quarter of its light, drawn in colours of its own that no theme reaches: a black strip, a seal, and its buttons.
It names the app it asks for, as the question gives it, and the client that asked, as the launcher names it: `mail, through factotum`.
While it is up, no window gets a key, a click or the pointer; the pointer moves only the cursor.
A click lands on a button only if the button went down and came up on it, half a second at least after the question showed, so that a click aimed at what was there before doesn't answer it; a key that answers waits the same half second.
Escape is deny.
A key's question has fields to type, a secret's drawn as bullets; tab goes to the next field, and return answers once.
Its text is what `/dev/kbd` says was typed, shifted as typed.
`pixel` reads the panel as black, so that what a client reads from the screen never holds it.

Radharc holds the clipboard: one entry, the representations of one thing, each a MIME type and its bytes, such as a text and an image of the same selection.
Radharc keeps a copy of the entry, so it lasts after the client that wrote it has gone, until another entry replaces it whole or Radharc stops.
Any client writes it: `snarf` takes text, as rio's does, and `clip/new` a whole entry; either is committed when the file is closed.
Only the client whose window is focused reads it, and only once that window has had a key or a press since it was focused, which a paste always has; a client in the background, or one watching the clipboard, is refused until then.
An entry marked secret, as a password field's copy is, is cleared 30 seconds after it was committed.

A client may also open a view, a window shown only inside another window, by a token: a channel end, which the window that shows it passes to `embed`.
A window may be fullscreen, place itself, be the desktop (beneath every other window, undecorated), a panel (above every other window) or a popup of another window; the window list, `windows` (radharc-windows(5)), lists them and moves, sizes, raises, hides and closes them.

The manifest:

```text
service windows
program radharc
resource mmio
resource system
bootdata
export per-client
use console
mount /dev kbdfs
mount /dev mouse after
mount /lib/theme bootfs/lib/theme
restart on-failure
```

# FILES

Each client has its own tree at `/svc/windows`; the files are the same in each.

: `status`
  Read only.
  A line each: the screen (`screen <w>x<h>`); the framebuffer (`display framebuffer <w>x<h> stride <s> <format>`); the input read (`input keys /dev/kbd, pointer /dev/mouse`, or `no keys`, `no pointer`); the vblank (`vblank synthetic <ns> ns`); the latch thread's profile (`latch deadline 4 ms of each 16.7 ms, admitted`, `refused (<reason>)` or `none (...)`); the frames latched (`latches <n>`); how late the latch woke and how long after its vblank the frame was out, over the last 120 frames (`late median <n> us max <n> us`, `shown ...`); the windows (`windows <n>`), the focused one (`focused <id>`), the frames composed (`frames <n>`), the pointer (`pointer <x> <y>`), the input routed (`keys <n> pointer events <n>`), and the prompt's questions queued, those asked since it started, and the clicks and keys that came too soon (`prompts <n> asked <n> ignored <n>`); the clipboard's entries committed since it started, and the reads it refused (`clip commits <n> refused <n>`).
: `list`
  Read only.
  A line a window, bottom to top: its id, its content's x, y, width and height on the screen, `focused` or `-`, and its title.
: `pixel`
  A write of `x y` chooses a pixel of the screen; a read gives the composed pixel there as six hex digits, `rrggbb`, or `outside`, and `000000` within the trusted prompt.
: `prompt`
  Only with `grant windows prompt`.
  Each open asks one question: a write of one ndb(6) entry, a read that waits for its answer, and gives it once.
  The entry's `ask=confirm` asks to allow something, and `ask=key` asks for a key's fields: each attribute written `name?` is a field, `!name?` a secret one.
  `app=` names the app it asks for (the asker if none); `answers=` the answers it offers besides deny, from `once`, `session` and `always` (`once` if none); its other attributes say what it asks about, shown as written, 256 bytes at most, and no secret.
  The answer is a line: the answer's word, and for a key its fields after it, `once user=jk !password=secret`.
  A write that isn't such an entry, or a second write, fails with `invalid`.
: `snarf`
  The clipboard's text.
  A read gives the entry's `text/plain` representation, or nothing if it has none; it fails with `denied` unless this client's window is focused and has had a key or a press since.
  Writes are collected, 1 MiB at most, and replace the clipboard with an entry of that text, `text/plain;charset=utf-8`, when the file is closed; closed without a write, it changes nothing.
: `clip/`
  The entry's representations, a file each, named by its type without parameters, lower case, with `/` written as `.` (`text.plain`, `image.png`), read as `snarf` is; with `new`.
  Listed, or walked into, by a client that may not read the clipboard, it holds only `new`.
: `clip/new`
  Write only: a whole entry, on one open.
  Each representation is a line, `type=<mime type> length=<n>`, and then its `n` bytes; the first line may add `secret=1`, an entry cleared 30 seconds after it is committed.
  An entry has 1 to 16 representations, no two of one name, 16 MiB in all at most, and `text/plain` 1 MiB at most.
  It replaces the clipboard when the file is closed, if what was written ends where a representation does; a write that breaks these rules fails with `invalid`, and the entry is not committed.
: `compositor`
  A service: radharc-compositor(5).
: `windows`
  A service: radharc-windows(5), the window list, its changes and its verbs.
: `theme/active`
  Read only.
  The theme in force: `theme <name>`, `variant <name>` or `variant none`, `generation <n>`, a line each, then a line `set <role> <colour>` for each role set over it.
: `theme/themes/`
  The installed themes, a file each, as `/lib/theme` holds them; the base is built in and isn't here.
: `theme/ctl`
  A write is one command: `load <name>` puts a theme in force, without a variant or roles set; `variant <name>` chooses a variant of it, and `variant none` none; `set <role> <colour>` sets a role over it until the next `load` (a colour as theme(6) writes one, or another role).
  A write refused fails with `invalid`, and a read gives the reason, as `theme check` words it, or nothing if the last command passed.
: `theme/events`
  Read only, and a read waits.
  Each reader gets its own lines from when it opened the file: `theme <name> variant <name> generation <n>` after each change, and `refused <reason>` for a command refused.
: `theme/resolved`
  Read only, memory: the theme in force, resolved, as one message in the wire format (radharc-themefile(5)).

